    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d727">

                <pb facs="0760" n="756" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg756"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6919: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-06-15T15:00:00+01:00">June 15, 1939—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 15—11:12 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1135. Embassy’s 1061, June 3, 3 p.m.; and 1107, June 10, 1 p.m.<anchor n="67"
                        xml:id="d727-f1"/> Chauvel tells me that the Foreign Office has received a
                    letter from the Ministry of Colonies to the effect that in view of the interest
                    of the United States in these tung oil shipments it has been decided to exempt
                    the shipments from payment of the Indo-China transit duty and to <pb facs="0761"
                        n="757" xml:id="pg757"/> provide the necessary railway facilities so that
                    the total amount of the shipments may be effected before the end of the
                    year.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
